---
import AppLayout from "../layouts/AppLayout.astro";

/**
 * `/guide` — the help a person needs to use metis, in their words. The full
 * model (and its engineering rationale) stays at /ontology for whoever
 * builds on it; this page links there rather than repeating it.
 */
---

<AppLayout title="Guide">
  <div style="flex:1; min-height:0; overflow-y:auto;">
    <article class="guide">
      <h1>How metis works</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Metis keeps one list of what your products can already do, so the next product starts from what
        exists instead of from zero. Four ideas carry everything else.
      </p>

      <dl class="terms">
        <div>
          <dt>Goal</dt>
          <dd>
            Why something exists. An <strong>org goal</strong> is the big aim; a <strong>product goal</strong>
            sits under one and says what a product is for. See them on <a href="/goals">Goals</a>; its
            <a href="/goals?view=network">Network</a> view links goals that rely on the same capabilities.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Capability</dt>
          <dd>
            Something we're able to do, named as a noun: <em>User authentication</em>, <em>Route planning</em>.
            Capabilities are shared: one capability can serve many products. They nest, so
            <em>Multi-factor auth</em> sits inside <em>User authentication</em>.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Feature</dt>
          <dd>
            What a person gets, named as an action: <em>Sign in with your company account</em>. Each feature
            belongs to a product and does one or more capabilities. A feature's status is planned, in progress or done.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Technology</dt>
          <dd>
            Something we could buy or adopt instead of building, such as a library, service or vendor. A technology
            can cover the same capability a feature does; a capability's page compares the two once technology data exists.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <h2>Where to start</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href="/home">Home</a>: describe an idea as a few features and pick the capabilities each one needs.
          Metis says which ones we already have, which only other companies' products have, and which nobody has.
          Save it as an idea to come back to. Home also scores every product, ours or not, and lists the most
          reused capabilities.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/products">Products</a>: one product at a time. <em>What it does</em> groups its features by
          capability; <em>Goals</em> says why it exists; <em>Plan</em> holds a spec, plan document and tickets, but
          only once someone asks for one. Products marked <em>external</em> belong to someone else; we record them
          to compare.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/journeys">Ideas &amp; journeys</a>: a journey is the path a person takes through features,
          possibly across products. An idea is a journey whose steps aren't built yet.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/capabilities">Capabilities</a>: the shared list, as a tree or most reused first, plus what needs
          attention. Each capability has one page: what it is, who uses it, why, and what depends on it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/features">Features</a>: every feature, as a list or a graph of what needs what. The
          <em>Plan</em> tab answers whole-graph questions: what's ready to start, the build order, and the smallest
          version that reaches a feature.
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href="/chat">Chat</a>: ask any of this in plain words. When Chat proposes a change, nothing happens
          until you approve it.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Getting products in</h2>
      <p>
        Metis only holds what your repos actually ship. A coding agent reads a repo and writes a seed file; you
        import it. On an empty instance, Home walks you through it; after that, it lives in
        <a href="/settings">Settings</a>. Technology and Stacks appear in the menu once technology data exists.
      </p>
      <p>
        Mapping a product by hand instead? Go in this order: create the product on
        <a href="/products">Products</a>, name the capabilities it uses on <a href="/capabilities">Capabilities</a>,
        add its features on <a href="/features">Features</a> and pick a capability for each, then say why:
        create a goal on <a href="/goals">Goals</a> and link it from each capability's page.
      </p>

      <h2>Reading the numbers</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Live</strong>: one of our products does this today. <strong>Buildable</strong>: every capability
          it needs is ours. <strong>Partly known</strong>: everything named so far is ours, but the list is
          incomplete. <strong>Missing</strong>: it needs a capability we don't have. <strong>Not described</strong>:
          nobody has named what it needs yet.
        </li>
        <li>
          A capability's feature count means <strong>our features, including those of its sub-capabilities</strong>.
          Products we don't own are listed separately and never counted.
        </li>
        <li>
          Coverage is a lower bound: a capability nobody has identified yet can't count as one we have. Names match
          exactly; a near name is offered as "did you mean", never assumed.
        </li>
        <li>
          Hover a status or relationship word to see the exact value metis stores, the same one Chat, agents and
          logs use.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who can do what</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Viewer</strong>: reads everything.</li>
        <li><strong>Editor</strong>: also changes products, features, capabilities, goals and journeys, and imports seed files.</li>
        <li><strong>Admin</strong>: also manages users, AI models and raw data, from Settings.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>For AI agents</h2>
      <p>
        Agents can ask metis the same questions over MCP, read only. See <code>docs/mcp.md</code> in the repository
        for how to connect one.
      </p>

      <p class="more">
        Building on metis or extending its model? The full specification, with its engineering rationale, is in
        the <a href="/ontology">ontology reference</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</AppLayout>

<style>
  .guide {
    max-width: 68ch;
    padding: var(--space-6) var(--space-6) var(--space-12);
    color: var(--text-body);
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    line-height: var(--leading-body);
  }
  h1 { font-size: var(--text-2xl); margin: 0 0 var(--space-3); }
  h2 { font-size: var(--text-xl); margin: var(--space-8) 0 var(--space-3); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--text-lg); color: var(--text-heading); margin: 0 0 var(--space-6); text-wrap: pretty; }
  .terms { display: grid; gap: var(--space-4); margin: 0; }
  .terms > div {
    display: grid; grid-template-columns: 9rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-4);
    padding-top: var(--space-4); border-top: 1px solid var(--border-hairline);
  }
  dt { font-weight: var(--weight-semibold); color: var(--text-heading); }
  dd { margin: 0; text-wrap: pretty; }
  ul { padding-left: var(--space-5); margin: 0; display: grid; gap: var(--space-2); }
  p { margin: 0 0 var(--space-3); }
  .more { margin-top: var(--space-8); color: var(--text-secondary); font-size: var(--text-sm); }
  @media (max-width: 560px) {
    .terms > div { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); gap: var(--space-1); }
  }
</style>
